{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788367966,"nonce":"IlHlpuzf8Kgt38juV0fvW/A7Hio=","title":"Evan, watching the bench on 2026-09-02 after `b7359fa` landed: *\"that's deterministic, it should tell you what's on the ground and next to that if it's forbidden or in a forbidden area — should this framework be anywhere else?\"*","message":"It should. Right now it is in exactly one file.\n\n\n`RimWorld/ForbidUtility.cs`:\n\n```csharp\npublic static bool IsForbidden(this Thing t, Pawn pawn) {\n  if (!CaresAboutForbidden(pawn, cellTarget: false)) return false;\n  if ((t.Spawned || t.SpawnedParentOrMe != pawn) \u0026\u0026 t.PositionHeld.IsForbidden(pawn)) return true;\n  if (t.IsForbidden(pawn.Faction) || t.IsForbidden(pawn.HostFaction)) return true;\n  ... lord.extraForbiddenThings, LordToil_Ritual.ReservedThings ...\n}\n```\n\nThe **cell** form is the one that consults `InAllowedArea`. So:\n\n- `IsForbidden(t, Faction)` — the `CompForbiddable` flag, nothing else.\n- `IsForbidden(t, Pawn)` — the flag **plus the allowed area**, plus lord and ritual reservations.\n- and separately `CanReserve` and reachability.\n\n\n**Pawn-scoped, area-aware — one file:** `DesignateReach.cs`, added by `b7359fa` this round.\n\n**Faction-scoped, blind to the area — everything else:**\n\n| file | what it answers | what a wrong answer costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Materials.cs` | build-material availability | **`place-layout --dry-run` reports `available: 30` for components no colonist can reach.** This is the number that made `e08c3e5` present as a material shortfall |\n| `BillIngredients.cs` | can this bill be worked | a corpse outside every allowed area reads as usable — **mechanism F2, the one that killed Marco**, in a second verb |\n| `FoodRot.cs` | how much food the colony has | `food_days` counts food nobody can walk to |\n| `ThingVerbs.cs` | `things` rollups | the same, everywhere a caller counts stock |\n| `StarterKit.cs`, `DesignateEngine.cs`, `TemperatureVerbs.cs` | assorted | smaller |\n\n`BillIngredients.cs` publishes `clauses_not_checked: [\"reachable\", \"reservable\"]`, which is honest — but the **area is not on that list**, because the file does not know it is missing it. That is the worst shape: a declared-complete answer with an undeclared hole.\n\n\n1. A single shared helper answers \"can a colonist use this thing\", pawn-scoped, quantified over the capable roster the way `DesignateReach` already does — who can, who cannot, and which area excludes the rest. **One implementation, not a second copy**: `DesignateReach.cs` already holds the guarded route, the `TrueCount == 0` short-circuit and the `EffectiveAreaRestrictionInPawnCurrentMap` guard, and those must not be re-derived.\n2. `Materials.cs` uses it. `place-layout --dry-run`'s `available` figure distinguishes \"on the map\" from \"reachable by someone who could build this\", and the preflight's shortfall wording follows. Coordinate with `e08c3e5`'s `no_builder`/`skill_blocked` vocabulary rather than inventing a third.\n3. `BillIngredients.cs` uses it, and `clauses_not_checked` either loses `area` or gains it explicitly.\n4. `FoodRot.cs` and `ThingVerbs.cs` either use it or state the scope in the envelope the way `food_rot` already states its stockpile/map-wide scope. **Stating the limit honestly is an acceptable answer** for the cheap read paths; silently answering the weaker question is not.\n5. Bench command list (markdown, not a Python suite): bind every colonist to a narrow area, leave material and a corpse outside it, and assert each surface above reports them as unusable rather than available.\n\n\nThis is a measurement-honesty issue, not a behaviour change: no verb should start refusing because of it. `33bc796` covers the `prioritize` half of the same idea and should be read alongside — it observes that `prioritize` is the one place that can already answer the pawn-scoped question and does not.","files":null}]}